## Marketing by stage

**For a faceless brand, the strongest marketing is proof and data: real demos, before/after case studies, and a quarterly "London Web Census" that local press will cover.** Paid ads wait until stage 3.

**Agency SEO, done safely.** Google's [June 2026 spam update](https://digitalshiftmedia.com/search-intelligence/google-june-2026-spam-update-home-services/) enforced its doorway-page and scaled-content rules \[V\]. Never publish 640 templated borough × niche pages.

- **Stage 1:** home, pricing, 5 niche pages ("websites for barbers"), portfolio, and a free "website check" tool (a PageSpeed wrapper that doubles as a lead magnet).
- **Stage 2:** add borough × niche pages only where you have a real client or demo there. Each carries a local case study, real screenshots and your own data ("we checked 412 Hackney salons; 51% have no site"). Cap at about 40 pages, adding 2 to 4 a month.

**Data-led PR (stage 2 onwards).** The pipeline already measures "% of businesses with no website by borough and niche". Publish it quarterly and pitch it to MyLondon, the Evening Standard business section, borough news, TechRadar and Small Business UK. TechRadar ran exactly this kind of story in November 2026.

**AI search visibility.** About half of local discovery now runs through AI tools, but AI recommends only about 1.2% of local businesses ([BrightLocal study](https://www.brightlocal.com/research/local-ai-visibility-study/), [Search Engine Land 2026](https://searchengineland.com/ai-local-visibility-report-2026-468085)) \[S\]. Citations come from a complete Google profile with recent reviews, presence on the directories AI tools crawl, clear schema with prices and FAQs, and third-party mentions.

- **For emtrc:** list on Clutch, DesignRush, GoodFirms, Sortlist, Bing Places and Apple Business Connect; publish explicit prices; reach 20+ Google reviews by end of stage 2.
- **As a product:** the "AI Visibility" add-on (Section 4).

**Faceless short video (stage 2, 3 a week, batched in one afternoon).** "We rebuilt this London café's website in 10 minutes with AI", "Rating London barbers' websites" (kind, never shaming), client wins. Tools: OBS or Screen Studio for recording, ElevenLabs or Gemini voice, CapCut, Veo/Gemini for B-roll, Buffer or Metricool to schedule. Treat it as trust (prospects check you after a walk-in) more than a lead source; content leads take 6 to 12 months.

**Case-study and review engine.** Every launch triggers: before/after screenshots, 3 questions to the client by WhatsApp, a 150-word case study, a portfolio page, a short video and a Google profile post, all done by a Claude skill. Ask for a Google review at launch and again at day 30 with results ("you got 14 calls from the site"). Paying for or incentivising reviews is banned under the DMCC Act 2024.

**Client newsletter (Brevo).** A monthly "your site this month" email: visits, calls and form leads from Cloudflare Analytics, one tip, one add-on offer. This is the main upsell and retention engine. Prospect newsletters go only to people who opted in.

**Paid ads (stage 3 only, capped at £300 to start).** UK small-business Google clicks average about £1.50 to £2.50, and London costs 15 to 30% more ([whito, Nov 2026](https://whito.co.uk/research/uk-ppc-costs/)) \[V\]. "Web design London" terms probably cost £4 to £12 a click \[K\], which works out to a £150 to £500 cost per client. Test long-tail terms ("barber website", "website for plumber London"). Use Meta only to retarget people who viewed their own demo (about £5 a day); average Facebook cost per lead is about $27 ([WordStream 2026](https://www.wordstream.com/blog/facebook-ads-benchmarks-2026)) \[S\].

**Events (stage 3).** A quarterly free "Get found on Google and AI" clinic with a BID or library business hub, run as a webinar or with a hired presenter to stay faceless.

